ask(c,'At fixed revolute angles, increasing downward d3 from80 to100 mm changes which tool coordinates?', ['Only z, which decreases by20 mm','Only z, which increases by20 mm','Only yaw, which increases by20°','Both x and y, each by20 mm'],['The FK equations place d3 only in z=d1−d3.','The selected coordinate is downward-positive, so height decreases.','A length change does not directly add an angle.','The horizontal coordinates contain only link lengths and revolute angles.'],kind='scenario',formula=[FK])
issue(616,'fk-arithmetic','Printed x228.6 and y369.7 do not match the given angles/lengths at the stated decimal precision.','Keep as-printed values distinguishable; use direct evaluated FK and final rounding for derived numerical questions.')
c=concept(617,'Inverse kinematics and branch feasibility','Cosine law, atan2 quadrants, both elbow branches and limits.',[616,617])
IK=fm('D=(x²+y²−l1²−l2²)/(2l1l2); θ2=±acos(D); k1=l1+l2 cosθ2; k2=l2 sinθ2; θ1=atan2(y,x)−atan2(k2,k1); d3=d1−z; θ4=φ−θ1−θ2',{'D':'cosine of the elbow angle','x,y,z':'target position in base frame','l1,l2':'positive horizontal link lengths','k1,k2':'equivalent planar-link components','θ1,θ2,θ4':'shoulder, elbow and wrist angles','d1':'fixed height','d3':'downward-positive extension','φ':'target yaw'},{'x,y,z,l1,l2,k1,k2,d1,d3':'mm','D':'dimensionless','θ1,θ2,θ4,φ':'radians, converted to degrees for reporting'},['Reachable planar target with|D|≤1; angle branches subsequently checked against limits and collision constraints.'])
ask(c,'For x=250 mm,y=150 mm,l1=250 mm and l2=200 mm, what cosine-law quantity D is used for the elbow angle?', ['−0.175','+0.175','0.85','−1.75'],['(85000−62500−40000)/100000=−17500/100000=−0.175.','This reverses the numerator’s sign.','0.85 is the squared radius divided by2l1l2 before subtracting squared lengths.','This loses a factor of10 in the denominator.'],kind='numerical',formula=[IK],steps=['x²+y²=85000 mm².','l1²+l2²=102500 mm² and2l1l2=100000 mm².','Divide−17500 by100000.'])
ask(c,'Which joint set is the positive-elbow branch for target(250,150,320) mm and yaw90°, with l1=250 mm,l2=200 mm,d1=400 mm?', ['θ1≈−11.5°, θ2≈100.1°, d3=80 mm, θ4≈1.4°','θ1≈73.4°, θ2≈100.1°, d3=80 mm, θ4≈−83.5°','θ1≈−11.5°, θ2≈−100.1°, d3=80 mm, θ4≈201.6°','θ1≈31.0°, θ2≈0°, d3=320 mm, θ4≈59.0°'],['atan2 branch evaluation gives approximately−11.5221°,100.0787°,80 mm,1.4435°.','The shoulder angle73.4496° belongs to the negative-elbow branch, not this elbow sign.','Changing only elbow sign without recomputing shoulder fails the position target.','This omits the elbow bend and confuses z with d3.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[IK],steps=['D=−0.175 givesθ2=100.078658°.','k1=215 mm andk2≈196.9137 mm.','θ1=atan2(150,250)−atan2(k2,215)=−11.522114°.','d3=400−320=80 mm;θ4=90−θ1−θ2=1.443456°.'])
ask(c,'For the same target and lengths, what is the other elbow branch before applying collision checks?', ['θ1≈73.4°, θ2≈−100.1°, θ4≈116.6°, with d3=80 mm','θ1≈11.5°, θ2≈−100.1°, θ4≈178.6°, with d3=80 mm','θ1≈−11.5°, θ2≈100.1°, θ4≈1.4°, with d3=−80 mm','θ1≈73.4°, θ2≈100.1°, θ4≈−83.5°, with d3=80 mm'],['Usingθ2=−100.078658° givesθ1=73.449627° andθ4=116.629031°.','The shoulder angle is not simply the negative of the first branch.','This is not the second elbow branch and reverses the required vertical displacement.','The elbow sign must change and the shoulder must be recomputed consistently.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',pages=[616,617],formula=[IK],steps=['Choose negativeθ2=−acos(−0.175).','Evaluate atan2 again with negativek2 to getθ1≈73.449627°.','Useθ4=90−θ1−θ2≈116.629031° andd3=80 mm.'])
ask(c,'Why does the shoulder solution use atan2 rather than a single arctangent of a ratio?', ['atan2 retains quadrant information from both signed components','atan2 eliminates all joint limits','atan2 makes unreachable targets reachable','atan2 avoids the need to account for the second link'],['Both the target direction and equivalent-link direction need correct quadrants.','Limits remain separate feasibility tests.','The cosine-law reachability condition still applies.','The second atan2 contains the second link’s contribution.'],difficulty='medium')
ask(c,'When is the statement “two IK sets exist” not generally valid?', ['At a branch-merging boundary, outside reach, or after limits remove a branch','Whenever the target has a nonzero y coordinate','Whenever the wrist yaw is not zero','Whenever the two link lengths differ'],['The two algebraic elbow branches can merge, become non-real or be excluded by constraints.','A nonzero y target can have two valid branches.','The wrist angle can compensate target yaw within its limits.','Unequal links still commonly allow two branches in the annular interior.'],difficulty='medium',conflict=(617,'Two possible sets of joint variables for a given(x,y,z,φ).','Two branches refer to generic reachable planar targets; boundary singularities, unreachable positions and joint limits change the feasible count.'))
c=concept(618,'Workspace envelope and joint-limit qualification','Annular diagram, volume, central hole and actual joint restrictions.')
WS=fm('Rmax=l1+l2; Rmin=|l1−l2|; V=π(Rmax²−Rmin²)h',{'Rmax,Rmin':'ideal outer and inner planar radii','l1,l2':'positive link lengths','V':'ideal annular-prism position-workspace volume','h':'available vertical stroke'},{'Rmax,Rmin,l1,l2,h':'common length unit: mm for radial examples, m for the volume example','V':'cube of the common length unit; m³ when all lengths use m'},['Full planar angular coverage; independent vertical stroke; ignore collision, joint limits and tool offset.'])
ask(c,'What do the red inner circle and green outer circle in the workspace diagram bound?', ['An ideal unreachable central hole and the maximum planar reach','Two distinct vertical stroke limits','The minimum and maximum wrist yaw','Two separate robot bases'],['The radii are|l1−l2| andl1+l2.','The diagram is a horizontal cross-section.','Angles are not the radial dimensions drawn.','Both circles are centered at the same base.'],kind='diagram',formula=[WS])
ask(c,'Ignoring joint limits, which radial interval is reachable with250 mm and200 mm links?', ['50–450 mm','0–450 mm','200–250 mm','50–250 mm'],['The ideal inner and outer radii are50 and450 mm.','The50 mm central hole is omitted.','These are individual link lengths, not the radial range.','The outer radius is the sum, not the longer link alone.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Rmin=|250−200|=50 mm.','Rmax=250+200=450 mm.'],formula=[WS])
ask(c,'Does the ideal annular result prove that every assembly point at radius at most450 mm is reachable?', ['No; points inside50 mm are excluded even before joint-limit and collision checks','Yes; the outer radius alone proves every interior point is reachable','Yes; the prismatic joint fills the central horizontal hole','No; no point between50 and450 mm can ever be reached'],['The stated requirement needs a lower radial bound and actual angular/vertical feasibility.','An annulus is not a disk.','Vertical motion does not change the horizontal radius.','The ideal annulus contains reachable positions.'],difficulty='medium',conflict=(618,'The arm must access all assembly locations up to450 mm and therefore satisfies the requirement.','The ideal radii leave a50 mm inner hole, and the joint limits on610 further restrict the envelope; outer reach alone is insufficient.'))
ask(c,'For ideal radii0.45 m and0.05 m and independent vertical stroke0.20 m, what is the annular-prism volume?', ['0.04π m³≈0.1257 m³','0.20π m³≈0.6283 m³','0.0405π m³≈0.1272 m³','0.08π m³≈0.2513 m³'],['π(0.2025−0.0025)×0.20=0.04π.','This is the planar area before multiplying by stroke.','This ignores the central hole.','This introduces an extra factor of2.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[WS],steps=['Square the radii:0.2025 and0.0025 m².','Subtract to obtain0.20 m² and multiply byπ.','Multiply by0.20 m stroke to obtain0.04π m³.'])
ask(c,'If the elbow is limited to±145° rather than reaching180°, what is the minimum radius for250 mm and200 mm links, ignoring other restrictions?', ['About143.5 mm','50 mm','450 mm','About291.5 mm'],['r²=l1²+l2²+2l1l2cos145° givesr≈143.474 mm.','50 mm requires a fully folded180° elbow, outside the stated limit.','450 mm is the fully extended maximum.','291.5 mm is the separate IK target radius.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',pages=[610,617,618],steps=['On|θ2|≤145°, the smallest cosine occurs at145°.','r²=62500+40000+100000cos145°≈20584.7956 mm².','Take the square root to obtain143.474 mm.'],assumptions=['Only the elbow bound is applied; base-angle availability and collisions may further restrict accessible directions.'])
issue(618,'workspace-sufficiency','Full annulus and all-points-up-to450 mm claims omit the central hole and contradict using±145° elbow travel for full folding.','Separate unrestricted envelope from joint-limited workspace and check radial/angular/vertical requirements.')
c=concept(619,'SCARA Jacobian and singularities','Derivative matrix, mixed units, determinant sign, velocity mapping and singular directions.')
JF=fm('v=J qdot; J=[[-l1s1-l2s12,-l2s12,0,0],[l1c1+l2c12,l2c12,0,0],[0,0,-1,0],[1,1,0,1]]',{'v':'task-rate vector(xdot,ydot,zdot,φdot)','qdot':'joint-rate vector(θ1dot,θ2dot,d3dot,θ4dot)','l1,l2':'horizontal link lengths','s1,c1':'sinθ1,cosθ1','s12,c12':'sin(θ1+θ2),cos(θ1+θ2)','J':'Jacobian for these coordinate orders'},{'l1,l2':'m','xdot,ydot,zdot,d3dot':'m/s','θ1dot,θ2dot,θ4dot,φdot':'rad/s','J':'mixed column/row units consistent with v=J qdot'},['Angles differentiated in radians; d3 positive downward; rigid geometry and tool origin on wrist axis.'])
ask(c,'Why is the prismatic entry J33=−1 in the displayed Jacobian?', ['Increasing downward d3 decreases upward tool height z','The prismatic joint reverses the elbow angle','Every prismatic Jacobian entry must be−1 regardless of coordinates','The wrist yaw decreases whenever d3 increases'],['It is the derivative ofz=d1−d3 with respect tod3.','The prismatic coordinate does not change elbow angle.','The sign depends on the coordinate convention.','Yaw is independent of d3 in this model.'],formula=[JF],pages=[615,619])
ask(c,'For the full4×4 Jacobian in the displayed row/column order, what determinant follows?', ['−l1l2 sinθ2','+l1l2 sinθ2','−l1l2 cosθ2','l1²+l2²'],['The planar2×2 determinant isl1l2sinθ2, multiplied by the−1 vertical entry and+1 yaw entry.','That is the planar minor, not the signed full determinant under this ordering.','Differentiation gives sine, not cosine, in the planar determinant.','This expression cannot detect the stated elbow singularities.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[JF],conflict=(619,'The slide writes det(J)=+l1l2sinθ2 below a4×4 matrix withJ33=−1.','Expanding the full determinant gives−l1l2sinθ2. The zero set is unaffected, so the listed extended/folded singular configurations remain valid geometrically.'))
ask(c,'Does the determinant sign correction change the singularity condition for positive link lengths?', ['No; both signs vanish when sinθ2=0','Yes; the singularity moves toθ2=90° only','Yes; every configuration becomes singular','No; because the determinant is never zero'],['Multiplying a determinant by−1 does not change its zeros.','The zero condition remains sine equal to zero.','The determinant is generally nonzero away from aligned links.','It does vanish at aligned configurations.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[JF])
ask(c,'At θ1=0°,θ2=90°,l1=0.25 m,l2=0.20 m, let qdot=(1,2,0.03,−3), with angular rates in rad/s and d3dot in m/s. What task-rate vector results?', ['(−0.60,0.25,−0.03,0)','(0.60,0.25,0.03,0)','(−0.20,0.65,−0.03,−3)','(−0.60,0.25,0.03,6)'],['J givesxdot=−0.2−0.4,ydot=0.25,zdot=−0.03 andφdot=1+2−3=0.','This reverses both horizontal and vertical signs.','This drops the second-joint x contribution and misuses the yaw sum.','The vertical sign and yaw sum are wrong.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[JF],steps=['At these angles the first two rows are[−0.2,−0.2,0,0] and[0.25,0,0,0].','Multiply by joint rates to obtain(−0.60,0.25) m/s.','Usezdot=−d3dot=−0.03 m/s andφdot=1+2−3=0 rad/s.'])
ask(c,'At θ1=θ2=0 with positive link lengths, which instantaneous Cartesian motion is unavailable through first-order joint motion?', ['Nonzero x velocity at the wrist origin','Nonzero y velocity at the wrist origin','Nonzero z velocity through the prismatic joint','Nonzero yaw rate through the wrist joint'],['The entire x row is zero at the straight configuration.','The y row contains nonzero link-length coefficients.','The vertical Jacobian entry remains−1.','The wrist yaw coefficient remains1.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[JF])
issue(619,'determinant-sign','The printed full Jacobian determinant has the opposite sign from its displayed matrix and coordinate order.','Use−l1l2sinθ2 for signed determinant; explain that the singular zero set is unchanged.')
persist()
# Dynamics and trajectory feasibility.
c=concept(620,'Generalized SCARA dynamics','Mixed effort units, inertia and velocity forces, and gravity loading.')
ask(c,'In τ=(τ1,τ2,F3,τ4), which component has units of force rather than torque?', ['F3, conjugate to the prismatic displacement','τ1, conjugate to the shoulder angle','τ2, conjugate to the elbow angle','τ4, conjugate to the wrist angle'],['A prismatic coordinate is paired with linear force in newtons.','Shoulder rotation is paired with torque in N m.','Elbow rotation is paired with torque in N m.','Wrist rotation is paired with torque in N m.'])
ask(c,'Which term in τ=M(q)qddot+V(q,qdot)+G(q) represents Coriolis and centrifugal effects?', ['V(q,qdot)','M(q) alone','G(q) alone','The coordinate q alone'],['The velocity-dependent generalized effort vector is V.','M maps acceleration to inertial effort.','G is the gravity loading vector.','A coordinate by itself is not an effort term.'],assumptions=['q denotes(θ1,θ2,d3,θ4); τ is generalized actuator effort.'])
ask(c,'Why can gravity loading vanish at the two horizontal SCARA revolute joints while their motor torques remain nonzero?', ['Their ideal motion does not change gravitational potential, but acceleration and velocity coupling still require torque','Zero gravity loading implies zero inertia','Horizontal links have no mass','The prismatic joint supplies every rotational acceleration torque'],['Gravity is only one part of the dynamics.','Inertia is independent of whether gravity produces torque about a given axis.','Horizontal links still have mass.','Rotational joints still need their own generalized actuator efforts.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[620,621,622])
c=concept(621,'Mass matrix structure and model assumptions','Symmetry, COM terms, rotational coupling, mixed units and evaluated entries.')
MF=fm('M11=I1+I2+I4+m1(l1/2)²+m2[l1²+(l2/2)²+l1l2 cosθ2]+m34[l1²+l2²+2l1l2 cosθ2]; M12=I2+I4+m2[(l2/2)²+(l1l2/2)cosθ2]+m34[l2²+l1l2 cosθ2]; M22=I2+I4+m2(l2/2)²+m34 l2²; M33=m34; M44=M14=M24=I4',{'Mij':'entry in the symmetric generalized inertia matrix for q=(θ1,θ2,d3,θ4)','I1,I2':'centroidal vertical-axis inertias of horizontal links1 and2','I4':'wrist vertical-axis rotational inertia','m1,m2':'horizontal link masses','m34':'m3+m4, distal translating mass','l1,l2':'horizontal link lengths','θ2':'elbow angle'},{'I1,I2,I4,M11,M12,M22,M44,M14,M24':'kg m²','m1,m2,m34,M33':'kg','l1,l2':'m','θ2':'rad'},['Horizontal COMs at half link lengths; distal masses share the wrist planar location; other omitted rotational inertias and motor/gear inertia are neglected as in this simplified model. M13=M23=M34=0.'])
ask(c,'Which structural property is visible in the printed generalized inertia matrix?', ['It is symmetric, with the prismatic coordinate uncoupled from the rotational coordinates in this model','It is antisymmetric, with opposite off-diagonal entries','Every off-diagonal entry is zero','Every diagonal entry has units of kg m²'],['Mij=Mji, and the third row/column has only M33.','Inertial matrices here are symmetric, not antisymmetric.','M12,M14 andM24 need not vanish.','M33 is a translational mass in kg.'],formula=[MF])
ask(c,'Why does the expression for M11 include m1(l1/2)² in addition to I1?', ['The link COM translates about the shoulder, adding a parallel-axis contribution','The term is the gravitational potential of link1','It removes the need to know link1 mass','It represents only wrist spin'],['I1 is the centroidal rotational inertia and the displaced COM contributes mr².','Potential energy would involve gravity and height, not this inertia term.','The term explicitly uses m1.','The first link’s COM motion is not wrist spin.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[MF])
ask(c,'For m1=1 kg,m2=2 kg,m3=m4=0.5 kg,l1=0.20 m,l2=0.16 m,I1=0.010,I2=0.020,I4=0.005 kg m² and θ2=90°, what is M11?', ['0.2034 kg m²','0.3314 kg m²','0.0634 kg m²','0.1934 kg m²'],['The contributions are0.035+0.010+0.0928+0.0656=0.2034.','This is the same model atcosθ2=1 rather than0.','This is M22 for the supplied data.','This omits m1(l1/2)²=0.010.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[MF],steps=['At90°,cosθ2=0 andm34=1 kg.','Sum inertias0.010+0.020+0.005=0.035 kg m².','Add1×0.1²=0.010,2×(0.20²+0.08²)=0.0928 and1×(0.20²+0.16²)=0.0656.','Total0.2034 kg m².'])
ask(c,'Using the same explicitly stated model with m2=2 kg,m3=m4=0.5 kg,l1=0.20 m,l2=0.16 m,I2=0.020,I4=0.005 kg m² and θ2=90°, which pair is (M12,M22)?', ['(0.0634,0.0634) kg m²','(0.1274,0.0634) kg m²','(0.0250,0.0250) kg m²','(0.0634,1.0000) kg m²'],['Atcosθ2=0 both equal0.025+2×0.08²+1×0.16²=0.0634.','M12=0.1274 belongs tocosθ2=1.','This drops all translational mass contributions.','1 kg is M33, not M22 in kg m².'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[MF],steps=['Setm34=1 andcos90°=0.','ComputeI2+I4=0.025,m2(l2/2)²=0.0128,m34l2²=0.0256.','Add them to get0.0634 for both entries.'])
ask(c,'In the displayed model, I4=0.005 kg m² and angular accelerations are θ1ddot=1,θ2ddot=2,θ4ddot=3 rad/s². Ignoring other wrist loads, what inertial wrist torque results?', ['0.030 N m','0.015 N m','0.005 N m','0.060 N m'],['The fourth row givesI4(1+2+3)=0.030.','This includes only the wrist’s relative acceleration and omits upstream contributions.','This includes only the shoulder contribution.','This doubles the sum without a stated factor.'],kind='numerical',formula=[MF],steps=['M41=M42=M44=I4 andM43=0.','Sum the three angular accelerations:6 rad/s².','Multiply by0.005 kg m² to get0.030 N m.'])
ask(c,'If m3+m4=1.2 kg, what is M33 and why is it not a rotary inertia?', ['1.2 kg; it multiplies linear acceleration d3ddot to produce force','1.2 kg m²; every matrix entry must have rotary-inertia units','0.6 kg; the two masses must always be averaged','1.44 kg²; the sum must be squared'],['The prismatic kinetic energy is0.5(m3+m4)d3dot².','Generalized coordinates mix angular and linear units.','Both translating masses contribute, so they are summed.','Mass is not squared in this kinetic-energy coefficient.'],formula=[MF])
c=concept(622,'Coriolis terms and gravity sign','Velocity coupling, zeros, downward-coordinate gravity and static support.')
VF=fm('h=l1l2(m2/2+m3+m4)sinθ2; V1=−h(2ω1ω2+ω2²); V2=hω1²; V3=V4=0; G3=−(m3+m4)g',{'h':'planar inertial coupling coefficient','l1,l2':'horizontal link lengths','m2,m3,m4':'link/distal masses in the source model','θ2':'elbow angle','ω1,ω2':'shoulder/elbow angular velocities','V1,V2':'velocity-dependent joint torques','V3':'prismatic velocity-dependent force','V4':'wrist velocity-dependent torque','G3':'gravity generalized effort for downward-positive d3','g':'positive gravitational acceleration magnitude'},{'h':'kg m²','l1,l2':'m','m2,m3,m4':'kg','θ2':'rad','ω1,ω2':'rad/s','V1,V2,V4':'N m','V3,G3':'N','g':'m/s²'},['Same simplified model as621; gravity vector is on the left-hand side of the equation of motion; d3 increases downward.'])
ask(c,'For h=0.064 kg m²,ω1=1 rad/s andω2=2 rad/s, which pair(V1,V2) follows?', ['(−0.512,+0.064) N m','(+0.512,−0.064) N m','(−0.256,+0.256) N m','(0,0) N m'],['V1=−0.064(4+4)=−0.512 andV2=0.064×1²=0.064.','Both signs are reversed.','This drops the cross term and usesω2 instead ofω1 forV2.','Nonzero velocities and nonzero h produce nonzero terms here.'],kind='numerical',formula=[VF],steps=['2ω1ω2+ω2²=4+4=8 s⁻².','V1=−0.064×8=−0.512 N m.','V2=0.064×1=0.064 N m.'])
ask(c,'If ω2=0 but ω1 is nonzero and h is nonzero, what do the source’s velocity terms predict?', ['V1=0 while V2=hω1²','Both V1 and V2 must be zero','V1=−hω1² while V2=0','V1=V2=hω1'],['The first expression containsω2 in each term; the second containsω1².','Shoulder motion can still induce the elbow centrifugal term.','This exchanges the two formulas.','The source terms are quadratic in angular rates, not linear here.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[VF])
ask(c,'With downward-positive d3 and z=d1−d3, why is G3=−(m3+m4)g?', ['Differentiating potential U=(m3+m4)g(d1−d3) with respect to d3 gives the negative sign','Gravity points upward in the base frame','The distal masses are negative','The motor effort is always positive during a stationary hold'],['The generalized gravity term is the potential gradient for the chosen coordinate.','Gravity physically points downward; the negative sign here is the compensating effort convention.','Physical masses remain positive.','A stationary hold requires upward force, negative in the downward-positive coordinate.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[VF])
ask(c,'For m3+m4=1.2 kg and g=9.81 m/s², what generalized prismatic actuator force holds the model stationary?', ['−11.772 N','+11.772 N','−1.2 N','0 N'],['At rest, acceleration and velocity terms vanish, leavingG3=−1.2×9.81.','This points downward in the stated coordinate rather than supporting the mass upward.','Mass must be multiplied by gravitational acceleration.','Gravity must still be balanced.'],kind='numerical',formula=[VF],steps=['Set joint velocities and accelerations to zero.','F3=G3=−(1.2)(9.81)=−11.772 N.'],assumptions=['No brake, counterbalance, external support or additional vertical load.'])
c=concept(623,'Trapezoidal motion and infeasible design numbers','Phase equations, endpoint assumptions, acceleration limits and speed-limited repair scenarios.')
TF=fm('θ=θ0+αt²/2 during acceleration; θ=θa+vp(t−ta) during cruise; θ=θf−α(T−t)²/2 during deceleration',{'θ':'joint position at time t','θ0,θf':'initial/final positions','θa':'position at end of acceleration','α':'positive acceleration magnitude','t':'time since move start','ta':'acceleration duration','T':'total move duration','vp':'positive cruise/peak velocity'},{'θ,θ0,θf,θa':'degrees for this example','α':'degrees/s²','t,ta,T':'s','vp':'degrees/s'},['Monotonic positive rest-to-rest motion; symmetric acceleration/deceleration; phase intervals chosen for continuity.'])
ask(c,'Which phase order describes the source’s trapezoidal velocity profile?', ['Constant positive acceleration, constant velocity, constant negative acceleration','Constant velocity, zero displacement, constant velocity','Constant positive acceleration throughout the entire move','Constant jerk, constant snap, constant velocity'],['These phases create rising, flat and falling velocity segments.','A dwell is not the stated middle phase.','Rest-to-rest motion needs deceleration.','The source describes piecewise acceleration, not a jerk/snap profile.'])
ask(c,'What assumption is required by the first-phase equation θ(t)=θ0+αt²/2 as printed?', ['Initial velocity is zero at t=0','Initial position must be zero','Acceleration must be zero','The time origin is the end of the move'],['Otherwise a nonzero initial-velocity termω0t is needed.','The formula explicitly permits nonzeroθ0.','A nonzero constant acceleration is represented byα.','The equation uses time from the acceleration phase’s start.'],formula=[TF])
ask(c,'Can a90° rest-to-rest move finish in0.38 s if |α|≤300°/s², even before applying the400°/s speed bound?', ['No; the maximum travel under that acceleration bound is10.83°','Yes;90/400=0.225 s already proves feasibility','Yes; the acceleration bound permits114° of travel','No; the acceleration bound permits no motion at all'],['The fastest acceleration-limited rest-to-rest profile has areaαT²/4=300×0.38²/4=10.83°.','Distance divided by maximum speed ignores acceleration and rest endpoints.','114°/s isαT, a velocity change, not the achievable displacement.','The bound permits positive travel, but not90°.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[TF],steps=['For fixed duration and bounded acceleration, use the triangular maximum-velocity envelope.','Peak velocity isαT/2=57°/s.','Travel is0.5T×peak=10.83°, below90°.'],conflict=(623,'The table simultaneously lists90° motion,300°/s² maximum acceleration,400°/s maximum speed and0.38 s move time.','Rest-to-rest travel is at mostαT²/4=10.83° under the stated acceleration cap. The listed90° move is therefore infeasible.'))
ask(c,'For a90° rest-to-rest move with maximum acceleration300°/s² and maximum speed400°/s, what is the minimum ideal move time and profile shape?', ['About1.095 s, triangular velocity','0.38 s, trapezoidal velocity','0.225 s, constant velocity from rest','About2.667 s, because the400°/s limit must always be reached'],['The peak√(αΔθ)=√27000≈164.3°/s is below400, soT=2√(90/300)≈1.095 s.','That time is inconsistent with the acceleration cap.','This ignores acceleration and deceleration.','A short move need not reach the maximum permitted speed.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[TF],steps=['Compute unconstrained triangular peak√(300×90)≈164.317°/s.','Since164.317<400, there is no cruise phase.','ComputeT=2×164.317/300≈1.095445 s.'])
ask(c,'In an explicitly revised90° move lasting0.38 s with a400°/s cruise speed, what symmetric acceleration magnitude is required?', ['About2580.65°/s²','300°/s²','About1052.63°/s²','About5261.58°/s²'],['Δθ=vp(T−ta) givesta=0.38−90/400=0.155 s, thenα=400/0.155.','This is the infeasible original bound.','400/0.38 accelerates over the whole move and leaves no deceleration allowance.','This uses only half of the required0.155 s ramp duration.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[TF],steps=['Solve90=400(0.38−ta), givingta=0.155 s.','Cruise duration0.38−2×0.155=0.07 s is nonnegative.','Requiredα=400/0.155≈2580.645°/s².'],assumptions=['This is a repaired scenario, not the printed300°/s² design; symmetric ramps and zero endpoint velocities.'])
issue(623,'infeasible-trajectory','The90°/0.38 s example cannot satisfy the stated300°/s² acceleration limit.','Distinguish printed data, acceleration-limited feasibility proof, minimum-time triangular solution and explicitly revised acceleration scenario.')
