ask(c,'Using the table’s0.5 kg part and0.8 kg gripper, what is the3 kg design-capacity-to-required-mass ratio?', ['30/13≈2.31','Exactly2.00','3/0.5=6.00','1.3/3≈0.433'],['Required mass is1.3 kg, so3/1.3=30/13.','The slide’s≈2 is a coarse description, not the exact ratio.','This ignores gripper mass.','This is the reciprocal ratio.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Add0.5+0.8=1.3 kg.','Divide3 by1.3 to obtain2.307692.'],assumptions=['Capacity-to-mass ratio only; not a complete dynamic or structural safety-factor certification.'])
ask(c,'The required stroke is50+80=130 mm and the chosen stroke is180 mm. What margin relative to the requirement does this represent?', ['(180−130)/130≈38.46%','20.00%','(180−130)/180≈27.78%','180/130≈138.46% extra'],['The excess50 mm is divided by the required130 mm.','20% would correspond to156 mm, not180 mm.','This uses the selected stroke as denominator, contrary to the requested definition.','138.46% is the total-to-required ratio, not the extra margin.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Compute excess stroke180−130=50 mm.','Divide50/130=5/13.','Multiply by100 to get38.4615%.'],conflict=(628,'The180 mm stroke is labelled approximately20% design margin over a130 mm requirement.','With the margin explicitly defined relative to the requirement, the result is38.46%;20% would give156 mm.'))
ask(c,'An ideal20-bit single-turn absolute encoder has how many distinct codes and what angle per code?', ['1,048,576 codes and about0.0003433°','20 codes and18°','1,048,576 codes and about0.3433°','524,288 codes and about0.0003433°'],['2²⁰=1,048,576 and360/2²⁰≈0.000343323°.','Bits encode binary combinations rather than one code per bit.','This is1000 times the calculated angle increment.','2¹⁹ is half the required code count and would double the increment.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Compute2²⁰=1,048,576.','Divide360° by this code count.'],assumptions=['Ideal uniform single-turn quantization at the shaft being measured; accuracy and repeatability are not inferred.'])
ask(c,'Does a20-bit encoder alone prove the ±0.02 mm robot-repeatability target?', ['No; transmission, compliance, calibration, control and measurement location also matter','Yes; bit depth uniquely determines Cartesian repeatability at every pose','Yes; all encoder codes correspond to equal Cartesian distances at every configuration','No; absolute encoders cannot be used for robot feedback'],['Encoder quantization is only one contributor to system performance.','A single hardware resolution value is not a complete error budget.','The Jacobian and mechanism geometry affect Cartesian sensitivity.','Absolute encoders are suitable feedback devices; the issue is the unsupported guarantee.'],difficulty='medium')
ask(c,'Which vision design choices are actually selected for the custom robot?', ['12 MP overhead camera,YOLO-based detection andPnP pose estimation','A force sensor alone for all visual defect detection','Four encoders used as the only image source','A barcode reader that directly replaces every kinematic transform'],['The requirements table explicitly lists the camera and two processing methods.','Force measurement does not supply the stated image-based inspection.','Encoders measure joint motion, not scene images.','Detected visual information still needs coordinate integration.'])
ask(c,'Which safety-related choice appears in the table, and what does that entry establish by itself?', ['Safety-rated encoder and speed monitoring are proposed; the row alone does not prove complete standards compliance','A normal camera alone proves all required safety functions','The20-bit resolution alone certifies the complete robot','The stated3 kg payload exempts the robot from all safety assessment'],['A proposed component/function choice is not a documented system-level conformity assessment.','An ordinary vision camera is not the listed safety choice or proof.','Bit depth does not establish a complete safety architecture.','Payload capacity alone does not establish an exemption.'],difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Educational interpretation of the supplied design table, not current legal or standards-compliance guidance.'])
ask(c,'The custom table chooses a0.38 s cycle below a0.5 s target. What additional information is needed before claiming the physical cycle can be achieved?', ['Actual path distances, accelerations, load limits, settling and process/dwell times','Only the fact that0.38 is numerically less than0.5','Only the wrist’s±180° travel range','Only the camera’s12 MP pixel count'],['Timing feasibility requires a motion and process budget, not just two target numbers.','The inequality compares targets but does not validate dynamics.','Travel range does not determine acceleration or process time.','Image resolution alone does not establish total cycle time.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[623,628])
